Canonicalization

The purpose of canonicalization is to minimize the ongoing effort required to maintain a system. A canonicalized system declares all state that must persist, making recovery a reconstruction from declared inputs rather than tracing past changes. Where multiple representations preserve specified behavior, one is selected, avoiding arbitrary variation that must otherwise be remembered and checked.

A canonicalized system:

- Is declaratively defined with NixOS and uses an ephemeral root filesystem, so the machine can be reconstructed from declarations and only declared state persists.
- Keeps its home directory in a Git repository with a whitelist `.gitignore`, making its versioned state explicit and reviewable.
- Organizes projects at paths derived from their Git remotes (for example, `github.com/owner/project`), so their locations can be discovered and checked without per-project configuration.
- Treats each project as a Git submodule and a Nix flake, allowing it to be versioned, initialized, rebuilt, and checked independently.
- Uses a restricted subset of the https://github.com/numtide/blueprint directory layout to make scaffolding and checking mechanical: each flake repository has a README explaining its purpose, packages with a single source file, and tests named for expected behavior.

`git canonicalization` helps create and maintain canonicalized home and flake repositories.

Initialize the home repository:

```sh
cd
git init
printf '*\n!/.gitignore\n!/.gitmodules\n' > .gitignore # add other required paths, such as .ssh, to the whitelist
git add .gitignore
git commit -m 'Initialize home repository'
```

Add and initialize a project submodule:

```sh
# Create the empty remote before starting this workflow.
git submodule add --force git@github.com:owner/demo.git ~/github.com/owner/demo
cd ~/github.com/owner/demo
git canonicalization init . # initializes a canonical flake repository
git canonicalization add <type> <name> # scaffolds a package in the current flake repository
nix fmt # runs conventional formatters and strict linters
nix flake check # verifies the canonical structure and runs builds and tests
```
